        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "16004580-e0c2-5b09-8cc0-4d87f99ab14a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Edward Garrison Walker, Charles Lewis Mitchell, David Walker, Eliza Walker\n- Places: Boston, Massachusetts\n- Roles: attorney, politician, artisan, abolitionist\n- Events: Massachusetts bar admission, Massachusetts State Legislature election, publication of \"An Appeal to the Coloured Citizens of the World\"\n\nFacts:\n- In 1829, David Walker lived in Boston, Massachusetts, and published \"An Appeal to the Coloured Citizens of the World,\" which called for black unity and self-help against oppression and injustice.\n- David Walker was an abolitionist and the father of Edward Garrison Walker.\n- Edward Garrison Walker, also known as Edwin Garrison Walker, was born in 1830 to Eliza Walker and David Walker.\n- Edward Garrison Walker was an American artisan in Boston before he became an attorney in 1861.\n- In 1861, Edward Garrison Walker became one of the first black men to pass the Massachusetts bar.\n- In 1866, nine years after Massachusetts extended the franchise to African-American men, Edward Garrison Walker and Charles Lewis Mitchell became the first two black men elected to the Massachusetts State Legislature.\n- Edward Garrison Walker later became a politician and died in 1901.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "2908fd18-99cf-51f1-8b2f-67af77e355e8",
        "text": "Page: Edward G. Walker. Sentence: Edward Garrison Walker, also known as Edwin Garrison Walker (1830–1901), was an American artisan in Boston who became an attorney in 1861; he was one of the first black men to pass the Massachusetts bar.\nPage: Edward G. Walker. Sentence: He later became a politician and in 1866, nine years after the state extended the franchise to African-American men, he and Charles Lewis Mitchell were the first two black men elected to the Massachusetts State Legislature.\nPage: Edward G. Walker. Sentence: Walker was the son of Eliza and David Walker, an abolitionist who had written an appeal in 1829 calling for the end of slavery.\nPage: David Walker (abolitionist). Sentence: In 1829, while living in Boston, Massachusetts, he published \"An Appeal to the Coloured Citizens of the World\", a call for black unity and self-help in the fight against oppression and injustice.",
